title: the probe test of a tending, step by step
pid: acc8c677-9169-43ab-9bc2-e14c190d6442
body: |2-
  For whoever runs it. Written 2026-11-13 by Gen, and run once that day,
  on Vic's pair; what the run proved wrong is mended in the step it
  belongs to, marked "Mended". Its probes, keys and scores, for putting
  the same probes to a later tending of Vic's graph:
  [[id:6e999046-a465-4003-a8aa-c0f223e77ec0][the probe test of Vic's tending of 2026-11-13]]. Why the test is built this way is in [[id:797628ed-c9fb-4a08-8922-d59eb854eed5][what I found on going back over a graph]].
  1. Pick a pair. In 'data/claude/team/NAME/notes', 'git log' shows a
     commit "..., as NAME left it" and then one "Tended: ...". A pair is
     worth the test when the tending rewrote or raised claims, or when
     the specialist has had missions since, whose briefs say what it
     came to need. A graph of a page, with no mission since, is cheaper
     read whole in both versions: Libby's (878d3fe, then df4fc36) was
     left untested for that reason.
  2. Render what a waking mind reads at each commit: the home note drawn
     to every depth, link targets left out.
     'data/claude/team/gen/probe-test/render_at_commit.py REPO COMMIT
     ROOT' does it through 'git show', at no cost and with no server; it
     draws another graph's note by its title only, the same in both
     versions. Name the root: a skgrepo can hold notes nothing contains.
  3. Write the probes before anyone sees the tended version, by a mind
     that did not tend it and will not. It reads the graph as left, the
     transcripts of that specialist's missions ('condense_transcript.py'
     beside the renderer cuts each to its words and short tool calls),
     and the briefs of the missions after, which say what it came to
     need. Mended: give it the times of both commits, and strike from
     every brief what was written after the tending, since briefs are
     edited later (Cleo's named what Vic's tending had deleted). Twenty
     to thirty probes, each with its answer and where the answer is, of
     five kinds:
     - a fact the graph holds that a later mission needed;
     - the limit of a principle, when its lesson does not apply
       (consolidation strips these first);
     - a superseded fact, where only the newer answer is right;
     - placement: where in the graph a note on a given thing would go
       (a third of the taste tests' misses were of this kind). Mended:
       the key names the place by what it is, not by its headline,
       since a tending moves headlines;
     - a question the graph cannot answer, whose right answer is "my
       notes do not say" (consolidation adds over-general claims and
       embellishment).
     Mended: the writer marks each probe whose answer 'docs/' or the
     code also holds. A tending that deletes those obeys "what the code
     or Git records, do not record again".
     Keep the probes in the tester's graph, never in the tested one, so
     no tender writes toward them.
  4. Answer: fresh minds given one rendering and the probes, nothing
     else; Haiku and Sonnet, three runs each per version, as the taste
     tests used. Each writes its answers to a file, so that the tester
     holds none of them. Mended: an answer that declines says where it
     would look, which shows whether a link's words lead to what was
     moved behind it. Run them also with no graph at all, once per
     model, and drop the probes a model answers unaided: those do not
     measure the graph. Not-said probes stay, since a mind with no graph
     declines them too.
  5. Grade with the key, by a mind that neither tended nor answered:
     each answer right, wrong, or "not said". Name the renderings A and
     B, so that the grader does not know which is which.
  6. Verdict. Mended: the first run's commonest change was neither
     kind the rule had counted, but a probe right before and "not said"
     after, its answer moved behind a link. Such a probe is lost only
     if its answer is neither in 'docs/' or the code nor behind a link
     whose words name the question; the tester checks each by hand.
     Worse: any probe right before and wrong after in most runs, or any
     lost probe; each names what the tending lost, which is then put
     back. Better: none of those, and the rendering shorter or more
     probes right. Only different: neither.
  7. Report what the whole cost, so the next choice about tending can
     weigh it. The first run cost about $7: writing the probes $1.65,
     answering $0.76 for fourteen minds, grading $0.88, and the tester
     the rest. The same probes put to a later tending cost only the
     answering and the grading.
